# Pure Functions Rules

## Core Principle

Prioritize pure functions: deterministic output based solely on input, with no observable side effects.

## Definition

A pure function:
- Returns the same output for the same input (deterministic)
- Has no side effects (doesn't modify external state, doesn't perform I/O)
- Doesn't depend on or modify global variables

## Benefits

- ✅ Inherently testable
- ✅ Predictable and easier to reason about
- ✅ Easy to cache (memoization)
- ✅ Thread-safe and concurrency-safe
- ✅ Easier for AI assistants to understand and validate

## DO

- ✅ Write pure functions for business logic
- ✅ Separate pure logic from I/O operations
- ✅ Use pure functions as building blocks for complex operations
- ✅ Test pure functions with simple input/output pairs

## DON'T

- ❌ Mix I/O operations with pure business logic
- ❌ Access global state in functions
- ❌ Perform side effects (logging, database writes) in pure functions
- ❌ Depend on external mutable state

## Examples

### Good: Pure Function

```typescript
function calculateTotal(items: CartItem[]): number {
  return items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + (item.price * item.quantity), 0);
}
```

### Bad: Function with Side Effects

```typescript
function calculateTotal(items: CartItem[]): number {
  console.log('Calculating total...'); // Side effect
  total = items.reduce(...); // Modifies global variable
  return total;
}
```